Q: How to implement dynamic style on HTML elements (leaflet map markers)? A: <p>A possible solution to try:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>:root {
   --found-opacity: 0.3; /* Default 30% */
}

.found {
   opacity: var(--found-opacity);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then to change the opacity dynamically:</p>
<pre><code>function updateFoundOpacity(newOpacity) {
    document.documentElement.style.setProperty('--found-opacity', newValue);
}
</code></pre>

Q: Why are buttons overlapping in a flex container? A: <p>The culprit is <code>position: absolute</code> on <code>.navbutton</code>. Absolutely positioned elements are removed from the normal document flow, which means the flexbox container can't see them — so all the buttons stack on top of each other at the same position.</p>
<p>Simply remove <code>position: absolute</code> from <code>.navbutton</code> and the flex layout will work as intended:</p>
<pre><code>.navbutton {
  color: white;
  font-size: 15px;
  text-decoration: none;
  background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.7);
  /* removed: position: absolute; */
  padding: 20px;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  border-radius: 9px;
  cursor: pointer;
  font-family: &quot;Courier New&quot;, Courier, &quot;Lucida Sans Typewriter&quot;, &quot;Lucida Console&quot;, monospace;
}
</code></pre>
<p>You might also want to add <code>width: 100%</code> to <code>.navbutton</code> so each button stretches to fill the container width, since buttons default to only being as wide as their content:</p>
<pre><code>.navbutton {
  width: 100%;
  /* rest of your styles */
}
</code></pre>
<p>That's also why plain text worked fine — text nodes are normal flow elements, so flexbox could arrange them. Buttons with <code>position: absolute</code> opted out of that flow entirely.</p>

Q: Set CSS @property with &lt;image&gt; syntax A: <p>A color is not an image. If you want a color to be an image make it a gradient with one color (a gradient is an image)</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@property --my-background {
  syntax: "&lt;image&gt;";
  inherits: true;
  initial-value: conic-gradient(oklab(0.91 -0.18 0.22) 0 0);
}

@property --my-colour {
  syntax: "&lt;color&gt;";
  inherits: true;
  initial-value: oklab(0.48 0 -0.31);
}

div {
  background: var(--my-background);
  color: var(--my-colour);
  font-size: 10vh;
  padding: 30px;
  text-align: center;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div&gt;this should be blue text on a green background&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: how to solve multiple threading in jpa repository save method A: <h3>Understanding the Race Condition in Multithreaded JPA</h3>
<p>The error occurs because of a <strong>Check-then-Act</strong> race condition combined with how Spring manages transactions.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Transaction Scope:</strong> The <code>@Transactional</code> annotation works via <code>ThreadLocal</code>. When you call <code>EXECUTOR.execute()</code>, the logic runs in a new thread where the original transaction is no longer present. Each thread is essentially acting independently.</li>
<li><strong>The Race Condition:</strong>
<ul>
<li><strong>Thread A</strong> checks <code>findById()</code> and sees no data.</li>
<li><strong>Thread B</strong> checks <code>findById()</code> at the same millisecond and also sees no data.</li>
<li><strong>Thread A</strong> performs the <code>save()</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Thread B</strong> attempts the <code>save()</code>, but because Thread A just committed that ID, the database throws a <code>Duplicate entry</code> violation.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<h3>Recommended Solutions</h3>
<h4>1. Database-Level Atomic Upsert (Best Performance)</h4>
<p>The most reliable way to handle this is to use a native &quot;Upsert&quot; (Update or Insert). This delegates the concurrency handling to the database engine.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@Modifying
@Query(value = &quot;INSERT INTO book (id, title) VALUES (:id, :title) &quot; +
               &quot;ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE title = VALUES(title)&quot;, nativeQuery = true)
void upsertBook(@Param(&quot;id&quot;) Long id, @Param(&quot;title&quot;) String title);
</code></pre>
<h4>2. Catch the Constraint Violation</h4>
<p>In a multithreaded environment, it is often better to &quot;ask for forgiveness than permission.&quot; Attempt the save, and if it fails due to a conflict, perform the update. Use <code>saveAndFlush()</code> to ensure the exception is thrown immediately within your try-block.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public void safeSave(Book book) {
    try {
        bookJpaRepository.saveAndFlush(book);
    } catch (DataIntegrityViolationException e) {
        // If a duplicate key exists, handle as an update
        bookJpaRepository.findById(book.getId()).ifPresent(existing -&gt; {
            existing.update(book);
            bookJpaRepository.save(existing);
        });
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h4>3. Handle Transactions Inside the Thread</h4>
<p>If you must use an <code>Executor</code>, ensure the <code>@Transactional</code> boundary starts <strong>inside</strong> the <code>run()</code> method of the thread, not on the parent method.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>// Move this to a separate Service
@Transactional
public void processInThread(Book book) {
    // Logic here is now protected by a transaction unique to this thread
    Book managedBook = bookJpaRepository.findById(book.getId())
        .map(existing -&gt; {
            existing.update(book);
            return existing;
        }).orElse(book);

    bookJpaRepository.save(managedBook);
}
</code></pre>
<h3>Summary of Best Practices</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Avoid <code>@Transactional</code> on methods that spawn threads:</strong> The context won't propagate.</li>
<li><strong>Use Unique Constraints:</strong> Your database schema is your final line of defense; always have a <code>PRIMARY KEY</code> or <code>UNIQUE</code> constraint.</li>
<li><strong>Prefer <code>saveAndFlush()</code>:</strong> In concurrent blocks, you need to know <em>immediately</em> if a SQL statement fails so you can catch the exception.</li>
</ul>
